  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1218 PM MDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nA large upper low will make \"landfall\" over southern California \nlater tonight. To our southeast, an upper level ridge is spinning \naway leaving us sandwiched in between the two systems. Anomalously \nhigh moisture is being pushed into the area. Showers and \nthunderstorms will pop up initially over the AZ/NM border during \nthe middle afternoon today and looks to continue into later \ntonight before fizzling away. Thunderstorms have the potential to \nbecome strong to severe today where damaging wind gusts (58+ mph) \nand/or severe hail (1\"+) is possible. A Marginal risk (Level 1/5) \nfor severe thunderstorms is in effect today encompassing the area \nalong and west of the Continental Divide. \n\nThe upper low starts to lift out over SoCal and southern NV area \nThursday afternoon. PW values will reach 1.1\"-1.2\" which would be in \nrecord breaking category. In addition, plenty of dynamics associated \nwith this upper trough where 30-40kt of 0-6km shear expected \nThursday afternoon. Scattered to numerous thunderstorms expected \ngenerally along and west of the Rio Grande. A Marginal Risk is in \neffect for the Rio Grande and west. A small area of a Slight Risk \n(Level 2/5) in portions of the Gila region tomorrow. Damaging wind \ngusts, severe hail, and even a small risk for a tornado or two \npossible Thursday afternoon generally along and west of the \nContinental Divide. A Flood Watch has been issued for the Gila \nregion Thursday starting at noon through 6 AM Friday morning. Models \nhave been focusing over this region for heavy accumulations around \n2\" or so. The anomalous moisture in the air will promote heavy \nrainfall in a short amount of time.\n\nA piece of energy pinches off of the main upper trough Friday which \nwill only continue the threat of flooding and severe weather. Drier \nair does look like it will have made it into far western portions of \nthe area but elsewhere will have a good chance to see thunderstorms. \nPWs look to drop to around 1\", which is still well above normal plus \n30-40kt of bulk shear. Flash flooding will continue to be a concern. \nAnother Marginal risk for severe weather is in effect for the \ncentral portions of the CWA Friday.\n\nHeading into Saturday, the second closed low will provide a source \nof lift for thunderstorms again focusing east of the Continental \nDivide. PWs do dip at this time to 0.8\"-1\" focusing out east, so \nflash flooding will still be a concern. Bulk shear will be around\n35-50kt that afternoon allowing for another round of severe \nweather likely. The second low finally starts to lift out Sunday \nmorning drying out much of the area from west to east. Some \nlingering storm chances Sunday for far eastern locations that \nafternoon.",
